WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:02.669
(Jim grunts)

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:06.506
-TORI: Nice.
-JIM: Our bushwack
has commenced!

00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:09.843
We want to make
our camp more like home,

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:13.263
so we're going foraging
for wood and supplies

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:14.681
to build some furniture.

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:17.642
But our camp here
is situated on protected land,

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:19.978
so we need to hike
to a spot upstream

00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:23.773
where the government allows
you to cut down dead trees

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:27.110
and, and live branches,
and basically source the wood

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.319
that we're gonna need.

00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:30.822
And we're bringing
the canoe on this bushwack,

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:35.368
so we'll have a way
to bring all this wood
back to our campsite.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.329
TORI: Oh God.
I think you're stuck, hon.

00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:41.124
-JIM: That's a no.
-TORI (off screen):
Reverse to your left.

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:43.585
-Turn the back.
-JIM: Yeah, I'm just
gonna go straight here.

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.004
(grunting)

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.633
TORI:
Carrying the canoe is definitely

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.178
an awkward
and uncomfortable
thing to do,

00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:54.596
with all the weight
on your shoulders,

00:00:54.596 --> 00:00:57.766
but he, also, can
put it down at any moment.

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:01.436
I'm carrying both children,
each weigh 30 pounds,

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:05.607
so... basically, wearing
a 60-pound weighted vest,

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:08.276
and I cannot take it off
and put it down for a moment,

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:11.071
but... I think
I have the cuter job.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.866
(heavy breathing)

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:16.409
I think this is our best bet.

00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:19.287
(Jim grunting)

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:23.541
-Hi. There we go.
-(babbling)

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:26.711
That's wonderful. Good job.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.883
I'm just noticing
how happy Wesley is,

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.303
and how much eye contact
he's been making with me.

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:38.389
And, um, eye contact
is something that he's not

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:40.058
always been so good at,

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:44.729
and... it's a big indicator
of some progression for him, so.

00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:49.692
Hi. There it is.
Hi, sweetheart.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.029
Yeah, I think this
is so good for him.

00:01:55.698 --> 00:01:57.826
-JIM (off screen):
Whew! We made it.
-TORI: All right. Ooh.

00:01:57.826 --> 00:01:59.828
(exhales)
Feel like I'm floating.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:04.499
Look at Wes sitting
so high in the canoe there
on his elbows.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:05.708
JIM: Come here, Wes.

00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:09.379
TORI: He was so happy
pretty much the entire walk,

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.965
like, when he wasn't sleeping,
he was, like, super happy.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.425
Also, just looking around
at his surroundings,

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:16.094
like, looking up at the trees.

00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:18.596
JIM:
That's really good, eh?
It just goes to show that

00:02:18.596 --> 00:02:21.307
it's even better for him to be
in the outdoors and in nature

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.268
-than your average person.
-TORI: Yeah.

00:02:23.268 --> 00:02:25.520
JIM:
So, you know, just that--

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.939
I don't know,
just different surroundings
or whatever it is,

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:29.482
it seems to be working for him.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.611
TORI:
It's fun to watch Hudson
interact with Wesley.

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:38.575
Hudson doesn't see
Wesley any differently
than he does Jim or I.

00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:42.579
JIM: I feel like it's
definitely helped Wesley

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.873
just have
another person there

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.332
that kinda he can connect with,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.500
even though his little brother

00:02:47.876 --> 00:02:50.170
pulls his hair sometimes,
it doesn't matter.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.713
-And I also think that...
-TORI: He bites.

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:54.257
JIM:
...that Hudson is
going to learn

00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:56.718
so much more responsibility,

00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:59.512
and, and be so much wiser
at such a young age

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.307
because of his
brother's challenges.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.435
Judging by the way
that the kids are behaving

00:03:05.435 --> 00:03:07.103
and how happy they are out here,

00:03:07.103 --> 00:03:09.022
it's making me think
that, uh, you know,

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:11.274
maybe we get
a little property
in a place like this,

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:12.859
and build a little
wilderness home.

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:17.655
Even if there's
potentially a place of buying

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:19.157
-a remote property in there...
-TORI: Mm-hm.

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:21.409
-Right.
-JIM: ...it could be
a, a possible place

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:24.954
that we might consider
building an off-grid cabin,

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:29.125
and slowly, you know,
basically starting to make
that our home base

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:32.503
'til one day, maybe that's
our permanent residence,
who knows?

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:34.339
-TORI: That would be amazing.
-JIM: Ah, yeah.

